Ambassador Dan Negrea
U.S. Representative to the United Nations Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.
The United States welcomes this historic moment: Syria’s first General Assembly resolution since the fall of the Assad regime. The United States generally opposes the creation of new international days in the UN system, as they add unnecessary costs pointless work to an already overloaded and costly UN agenda, while achieving nothing of note. Our support for this resolution reflects the uniqueness of both the situation of missing persons and those who are searching for them, and the new chapter that has begun in a country long ravaged by a brutal civil war.
Colleagues, the issue of missing persons is of the utmost importance, not just to the family members and loved ones of those missing, but to communities and even nations. Thousands are still missing in Syria, in Colombia, in many countries around the world. Resolving these cases is imperative not only to bring some measure of closure to individuals involved but also to promote justice and accountability.
###
I would like to recognize the searchers of missing persons in attendance here today, including U.S. citizens whose advocacy on behalf of their missing family members has helped to raise awareness and keep this issue on the agenda here at the United Nations. Thank you for your tireless dedication.
The United States strongly supports the mandate of the Independent Institution on Missing Persons (IIMP – double-I-M-P) in Syria and encourages the Syrian government to continue coordination with the IIMP to ensure accountability for the families and accomplish the shared goal of finding and identifying the remaining missing. We urge the IIMP and International, Impartial, and Independent Mechanism (triple-I-M) to work with the Syrian government to relocate staff and offices to Damascus and to finalize MOUs that will enable them to provide resources to support the ongoing transitional justice project in Syria.
As we look ahead to next month’s General Assembly high-level week, we encourage all UN member states to seek meaningful engagement with the Syrian government in support of a stable, secure, and prosperous Syria. We applaud the progress the Syrian authorities continue to make on political, humanitarian, and security priorities.
