Thank you, Madam President, and thank you to today’s briefers, of course.
Colleagues, the United States rescinded its designation of Syria as a State Sponsor of Terrorism on August 24, a historic milestone that recognizes the progress the Syrian authorities continue to make on political, humanitarian, and security priorities.
Lifting U.S. restrictions on Syria will continue to unlock international trade and investment opportunities, contributing to the rebuilding of Syria. A stable, unified Syria at peace with itself and its neighbors will also enhance peace and security for those in the region and beyond.
We look forward to further supporting Syria’s counterterrorism efforts by building on its membership in the Global Coalition to Defeat ISIS.
The United States also applauds Iraq and Syria’s July 17 announcement to advance rehabilitation of the Iraq-Syria crude oil pipeline, restoring a critical energy corridor which will make energy more accessible to the global market.
We welcome the engagement of a U.S.-led consortium on this project, which advances security and stability through prosperity for both countries.
This progress reflects the vision and leadership of President Trump to build a more prosperous and interconnected region.
We reiterate our call for UN institutions and Member States to support the Syrian government’s efforts to advance these goals. We urge the International, Impartial and Independent Mechanism (IIIM) and the Independent Institution on Missing Persons in Syria (IIMP) to work with the Syrian government to finalize MOUs that will enable both of those parties and Syria to provide resources to support the ongoing transitional justice project in Syria.
We take note of the IAEA’s productive recent visit to Syria and welcome cooperation between the Syrian government and the IAEA toward fulfilling Syria’s safeguard obligations related to the Treaty of the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons in a safe and expeditious manner.
We also call on both Israel and Syria to seek de-escalation in the aftermath of the August 18 airstrikes on the Abu al-Duhur Airbase. The United States will continue to engage closely with both countries and with all regional stakeholders to promote dialogue and peaceful solutions.
As we look ahead to High-Level Week next month, we encourage members of this Council and all Member States to use this opportunity for meaningful engagement with the Syrian government in support of a stable, secure, and prosperous Syria.
Thank you.
