Thank you, Mister President, and thank you to Denmark for convening this important meeting. Also, thank you to all of the briefers this morning.
When this Council adopted Resolution 2417 in 2018, the Trump Administration reiterated the United States’ commitment to help feeding the hungry. The United States remains committed to that goal.
Unfortunately, conflicts and crises have grown in scale and complexity. Food insecurity is rising, and the humanitarian systems to address food insecurity are struggling to keep pace.
And this was before the Iranian regime’s attacks in the Strait of Hormuz, obstructing nearly one-quarter of global oil trade and roughly one-third of the global fertilizer trade.
We are outraged that the Iranian regime continues its egregious and unconscionable efforts to disrupt freedom of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz.
In Resolution 2417, this Council demanded that parties to conflict not impede humanitarian assistance. Today, Iran’s actions are felt all over the world. The World Food Programme now projects an additional 45 million people will suffer from acute food insecurity globally.
The Iranian regime has spiked the cost of fuel by blocking exports through the Strait. Increased energy costs hurt everyone. But in places like Syria and Gaza, increased costs threaten the precarious food security gains made by humanitarian actors and the communities they serve.
The fertilizers that Iran prevents from passing through the Strait of Hormuz are necessary for food production in sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia, and parts of Southeast Asia. The projected loss in agricultural outputs in the coming months mean less food, higher prices, and more hunger in places like Sudan, Nigeria, and the DRC.
In the DRC alone, more than 16 million people need food assistance, according to the Famine Early Warning Systems Network. Armed groups continue to attack civilians and block humanitarian routes while the Ebola outbreak threatens millions more.
In Sudan, conflict drives the world’s largest humanitarian crisis and parties to the conflict have reportedly used food access and availability as a war tactic. We call on all parties to this conflict to facilitate rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access throughout Sudan.
But conflict is not the only threat to global food security. Natural phenomena like El Niño risk pushing tens of millions into acute hunger.
The United States continues to partner with humanitarian organizations and governments to address—and where possible, preempt—food insecurity. To that end, we recently announced more than $1 billion in U.S. assistance to UNICEF and WFP to address global humanitarian, nutrition, and food security needs.
We are also actively pursuing options to respond to fertilizer shortages.
The United States will not abandon the needy or leave them to suffer hunger, whether the result of conflicts or natural disasters.
Thank you.
