Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President, and to our briefers today—with a particular thanks to Special Representative Tetteh for her continued leadership of UNSMIL.
Important work remains to reach durable, nationwide unity. The United States continues to be encouraged by the trajectory of the Libyan-led political process and UNSMIL’s roadmap.
We welcome the conclusion of UNSMIL’s nationwide Structured Dialogue, as a meaningful step forward in a Libyan-owned process.
The United States is proud of the substantial progress achieved this summer through our sustained, hands-on engagement with Libyan leaders from east to west.
We have deepened engagement with Libyan stakeholders across the country, underscoring that unification must serve all Libyans and preserve Libya’s sovereignty.
We convened senior officials from both regions in Malta in July to advance concrete unification efforts.
And, after a U.S. Congressional delegation visited Libya earlier this summer, Libyan defense leaders committed to three historic and tangible steps towards military unification: establishing a Joint Operations Center in Sirte, an Air Force Training Center of Excellence in Misrata, and an Integrated Air Command.
The United States continues to support full implementation of Libya’s first unified national budget in 13 years. And, we have engaged directly with Libyan economic leaders to strengthen investment and the business climate.
These U.S. efforts are fully complementary to UNSMIL’s roadmap.
On sanctions, the United States works closely with the 1970 Committee Panel of Experts to support their reporting on sanctions evasion.
We look forward to the Panel’s next report on armed groups’ control of oil revenue and the increase of oil and fuel smuggling.
Real momentum exists. We urge continued Libyan and international support to turn this progress into lasting peace and unified institutions.
Thank you.
