New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President, and thank you to our briefers this morning.
As the United States has said many times, we strongly condemn violence by any party in the West Bank.
The United States appreciates the Israeli government’s condemnation of, and the increased actions to counter, violence in the West Bank. Israel has taken specific steps, such as increasing law enforcement actions to boosting the IDF presence, and to allocating significant financial resources to counter the violence.
The United States will continue to work constructively to increase security, reduce tensions, and enhance stability that is beneficial for both Israelis and Palestinians alike.
President Trump’s 20-Point Plan to End the Gaza Conflict makes clear that the PA must verifiably complete a slate of reforms. The United States will not accept compensation for terrorism under the guise of “welfare” or through any other attempts to evade detection under a different name.
The United States reiterates its opposition to annexation of the West Bank.
The Security Council must continue to show our strong support to the Board of Peace and the National Committee for the Administration of Gaza. They are working to advance President Trump’s Peace Plan, which this Council endorsed in its historic Resolution 2803 last November.
We also welcome the progress that the Board and the NCAG have achieved so far. This includes boosting humanitarian assistance with safeguards in place to minimize Hamas’ stealing and looting of resources.
We further welcome the milestone achieved at the end of July, when Hamas finally and for the first time committed to an actionable plan for its disarmament.
All parties must build on this momentum to full and irreversible disarmament and ensure that Gaza is never again a launching pad for threats to Israel’s national security. This path forward, as the Board of Peace has noted, “holds the promise of delivering significant benefits to the people of Gaza.”
Thank you.
