Ambassador Dan Negrea
Representative of the United States to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chair. I want to thank Latvia and the UK for organizing this Arria and DPR Pavlichenko for his remarks. I would like to thank, Assistant Secretary-General Fuentes Julio, Mr. Garsiya, Ms. Umerova, Mr. Gushchyn, and Ms. Keiran for their briefings.
We have closely followed the reports of foreign nationals from the Middle East and Africa being coerced or recruited under false pretenses into the Russian Armed Forces.
We are also aware of credible reports that thousands of Cubans have traveled to Russia to fight in Ukraine. Yet again, the Cuban regime has failed to protect its citizens – this time, from being used as pawns in the Russia-Ukraine war – a war whose cost continues to mount.
President Trump has condemned the humanitarian toll of the war and we echo his calls for immediate ceasefire and a return to negotiated settlement.
We witnessed concrete results in April and May, when Russia and Ukraine agreed to exchange hundreds of prisoners and additional civilians. The United States supports such exchanges as steps towards durable peace and calls for continued efforts to return both civilians and prisoners of war to their home countries.
As a further step, the United States will continue to work with the governments of Russia and Ukraine to safely return children to their families and communities.
The United States’ dedication to the safe return of children is unwavering. On July 30, First Lady Melania Trump helped reunite a fifth group of Ukrainian and Russian children with their loved ones, continuing her humanitarian work on behalf of those affected by the war.
Concurrently, the United States continues to support a $25 million assistance program to aid the return of Ukrainian children forcibly transferred to Russia and Russian-controlled territories.
The United States will continue to do whatever possible to reunite families torn apart by war and support the negotiations and confidence-building measures necessary to achieve lasting peace. Thank you.
